  {
   "id": "iWqM8T0FAgAJ",
   "from": "Jeremy Miles",
   "author": "0325c6a976085eab",
   "date": "2025-09-09T20:08:24Z",
   "subject": "Re: SAVE THE DATE: The International Conference on Multilevel Analysis",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Tue, 9 Sept 2025 at 12:52, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">cff...@gmail.com</a> &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">cff...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">Thanks Terrence! I wonder, how come there&#39;s no journal of multilevel analysis?<br><br></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>(Not Terrence here). I remember Jack McArdle many years ago saying  that there shouldn&#39;t be a journal &quot;Structural Equation Modelling&quot; - there&#39;s no Journal of ANOVA, or  Journal of Regression. It creates divisions - where does SEM start, and multilevel end, and multilevel start and IRT end. Journals should be focused on problems, not solutions to problems (is my poor attempt to paraphrase what he said).</div><div><br></div><div>The other answer is that there&#39;s no journal of multilevel analysis, because no one has made one. In the world of open access online journals, starting a journal is many times easier than it used to be. But it is still hard work to start it, and hard work to keep it going. George Marcoulides was the first editor of the SEM journal (in 1994), and I would imagine did a huge amount of work to get it going, and keep it going. He is currently the editor (I think he has been the editor the whole time, but I&#39;m not sure). I&#39;ve been an associate editor for a  couple of journals for a 5 year term, and I found that pretty exhausting. I can&#39;t imagine being the editor for more than 30 years.</div><div><br></div><div>Jeremy</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "bGiVmuEVAgAJ",
   "from": "Shu Fai Cheung (張樹輝)",
   "author": "bc810b1d0900407f",
   "date": "2025-09-10T01:13:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: SAVE THE DATE: The International Conference on Multilevel Analysis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>I recall there was a newsletter for multilevel modeling. I should have read some of them, but it&#39;s the days when we still used floppy disks to store things. I couldn&#39;t find any of their, if any, on my computer. Maybe there are some hard copies of them somewhere in the (physical) file cabinets that I rarely open now. :)</div><div><br></div><div>I searched, and this is the first entry I found, which seems to be the last edition/issue of the newsletter:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://www.bristol.ac.uk/media-library/sites/cmm/migrated/documents/new18-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.bristol.ac.uk/media-library/sites/cmm/migrated/documents/new18-1.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792412855246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2eD2pkLEksFdgQ1mFWBu4n\">https://www.bristol.ac.uk/media-library/sites/cmm/migrated/documents/new18-1.pdf</a></div><div><br></div><div>-- Shu Fai</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><br><div><div dir=\"auto\">On Wednesday, September 10, 2025 at 4:08:24 AM UTC+8 <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jeremy...@gmail.com</a> wrote:<br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Tue, 9 Sept 2025 at 12:52, <a rel=\"nofollow\">cff...@gmail.com</a> &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">cff...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">Thanks Terrence! I wonder, how come there&#39;s no journal of multilevel analysis?<br><br></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>(Not Terrence here). I remember Jack McArdle many years ago saying  that there shouldn&#39;t be a journal &quot;Structural Equation Modelling&quot; - there&#39;s no Journal of ANOVA, or  Journal of Regression. It creates divisions - where does SEM start, and multilevel end, and multilevel start and IRT end. Journals should be focused on problems, not solutions to problems (is my poor attempt to paraphrase what he said).</div><div><br></div><div>The other answer is that there&#39;s no journal of multilevel analysis, because no one has made one. In the world of open access online journals, starting a journal is many times easier than it used to be. But it is still hard work to start it, and hard work to keep it going. George Marcoulides was the first editor of the SEM journal (in 1994), and I would imagine did a huge amount of work to get it going, and keep it going. He is currently the editor (I think he has been the editor the whole time, but I&#39;m not sure). I&#39;ve been an associate editor for a  couple of journals for a 5 year term, and I found that pretty exhausting. I can&#39;t imagine being the editor for more than 30 years.</div><div><br></div><div>Jeremy</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div>\r\n</blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
